\section{Compile the Operations That Resolution Keeps Repeating}

A source interpreter can copy fresh clauses, inspect their syntax, and maintain explicit substitutions at each step. Holm's \emph{Logic Programming in Scheme} is an example of building up a logic engine around substitutions as data. A Prolog implementation can instead compile head unification and body calls into instructions over mutable term cells. The Warren Abstract Machine (WAM) is the classic design. Diehl, Hartel, and Sestoft's survey \emph{Abstract machines for programming language implementation}, section~7, describes its memory areas and specialized instructions. This chapter follows the machine's obligations, not a specific implementation's bytecode.

Consider this Prolog lowering pass for an imaginary instruction set:

\begin{verbatim}
encoding(add, short).
encoding(add, long).
encoding(sub, long).

enabled(long).

lower(Op, Code) :-
    encoding(Op, Code),
    enabled(Code).
\end{verbatim}

The query \verb|?- lower(add, Code).| tries \texttt{short}, rejects it in \texttt{enabled/1}, and then returns \verb|Code = long|. Luger and Stubblefield's account of Prolog backtracking explains the source behavior; the machine must implement that behavior without copying the entire heap each time it tests an encoding.

\section{A Continuation Is Not a Choice Point}

Argument registers carry terms into a predicate. Head instructions inspect or unify them: a constant in a clause can bind an unbound cell or compare against an already-bound value. Compound-term instructions can read an existing structure or construct one when the actual argument is a variable. Variables that only need to survive within an instruction sequence can live in temporary registers. Values needed across a non-tail call have to survive register reuse; a conventional WAM compilation assigns such permanent variables to an environment, which also saves the ordinary continuation. In the example, the reference for \texttt{Code} must remain available across \texttt{encoding/2} so that \texttt{enabled/1} can inspect it. Being permanent in this sense is a liveness property, not an exemption from backtracking.

A continuation says where to resume if a call succeeds. A choice point says where to go if a branch fails or the caller requests another answer. A choice point captures the next alternative and the state needed to resume there, including arguments, relevant control registers, and heap and trail boundaries. A heap holds allocated variables and structures; the trail logs variable cells that must be reset on retry. The survey also describes a push-down list of pairs awaiting unification. That list is local work for solving term equations, not a list of untried clauses. \Cref{fig:visual-342} shows the two roles played by the choice point and trail in this example.

