\section{Walkthrough: Restore the State Before Retrying}

The pseudocode models retry at one choice point; finishing its final alternative also removes that choice point. It assumes binding entries for cells that were unbound before modification, as in ordinary variable trailing.

\begin{verbatim}
bind_unbound(cell, value):
    if cell survives rollback to the latest choice:
        trail.push(cell)                 # its binding must not survive
    cell := value

retry(choice):
    while trail.top > choice.trail_top:
        cell := trail.pop()
        reset_to_unbound(cell)            # before reusing memory
    restore_heap_top(choice.heap_top)
    restore_arguments_and_control(choice.saved_state)
    jump to choice.next_alternative
\end{verbatim}

At entry to \texttt{encoding/2}, the query's \texttt{Code} cell already exists. Binding it to \texttt{short} therefore records that address on the trail. The call \texttt{enabled(short)} fails, retry unbinds \texttt{Code}, and the second head binds it to \texttt{long}. The invariant at retry is that all cells that existed at the saved point again have the bindings they had there, and younger heap allocations are no longer in use by the restored computation. Heap restoration suffices for cells allocated after the choice; trailing all such cells would add unnecessary work. The machine must also respect storage lifetimes: a surviving reference cannot point into reclaimed stack or heap space.

\section{Optimize Predicate Entry Without Changing the Search}

First-argument indexing can skip the \texttt{sub} fact for the query with \texttt{add} known. It must permit the appropriate candidates when the argument is unbound and, for ordinary Prolog predicates, preserve the order of remaining alternatives. Last-call optimization can reuse or drop an environment when no local continuation is needed and doing so is safe with outstanding choices. A direct cut can discard choice points newer than its entry boundary; it must not undo the current successful bindings. These optimizations change the work the engine performs, not the required order or scope of retry. A regression test should force the first head to bind an older cell and a body goal to fail: if the second head then appears not to match, the problem may be a missing trail entry rather than clause selection.

Thus the compiled engine is itself a compiler component: its instruction selection may change allocation and probe costs, but its observable output remains the source-level sequence of answers, failures, and permitted retries.
